In the normal state we think of what we are going to write before the act of writing begins. There is a direct action of the will in causing the pen, the hand, and the fore-arm to move over the paper. In the abnormal state, on the other hand, we do not think before writing;
we do not move the hand,
but let it remain inert,
passive,
free;
we place it upon the paper,
taking care merely that it shall meet with the least possible resistance;
we think of a word,
a figure,
a stroke of the pen,

